/*
 * The main page of the API reference and the page on the x3p format, which the
 * documentation of the single functions refers to.
 */

/**
 * @mainpage libx3pio
 *
 * libx3pio is a portable C library that reads and writes the x3p files of
 * ISO 25178-72. An x3p file is a zip container that holds the points of a
 * profile, of an areal surface measurement or of a point cloud, and the
 * records about them in XML. It is used as an interchange format for
 * topography data and as a software measurement standard.
 *
 * @section features Features
 * - Implemented according to ISO 25178-72:2017 and its Amendment 1:2020, with
 *   the revisions `ISO5436 - 2000` and `ISO25178-72:2017/DAM1`.
 * - Profiles, surfaces with layers and point clouds; axes that are incremental
 *   or absolute; the data types int16, int32, float and double; the binary and
 *   the text format of the points; vendor-specific files.
 * - Application-side buffers with arbitrary strides: a reader writes into, and
 *   a writer reads from, the memory of the program, also members of structures
 *   and columns of matrices.
 * - A point cloud, which is a list of points, is read and written in any number
 *   of parts, so a program needs the memory of one part and not of the whole
 *   cloud.
 * - Access to the points as numbers in metres, in the coordinate system of the
 *   file or in the global one that its transformation gives, or as the values that
 *   the file stores, together with the mask of the points that were
 *   measured.
 * - Files and blocks of memory as sources, and files and memory as targets. A
 *   reader reads only the member of the zip file that a call needs.
 *   A path is UTF-8 on every platform; on Windows x3pio_open_wfile() and
 *   x3pio_create_wfile() take paths of wide characters.
 * - Thread support: the library holds no global state, so threads use handles
 *   of their own at the same time.
 * - The headers of the library do not include those of its dependencies, so a
 *   program needs neither minizip-ng nor Expat to compile.
 *
 * @section limitations Limitations
 * - A handle must not be shared between threads.
 * - Points can be read only once and in the order of the file; a writer takes
 *   them in the same order.
 * - The number of points must be known before the first one is written, since
 *   the document is part of the file.
 * - After a failure while the points are read or written, the handle refuses
 *   further calls.
 * - The library allocates with `malloc`; it has no custom allocators, since the
 *   zip libraries it builds on could not honor them.
 * - The library is not meant for microcontroller firmware: the zip container
 *   needs a heap, a file system or a stream, and a deflate implementation. It
 *   targets hosts and embedded Linux.
 * - Encrypted members and containers of more than one volume are not
 *   supported.
 *
 * @section building_build Building and Installing
 * The library requires a C11 compiler, CMake, minizip-ng (4.0 or later) built
 * with zlib, which the deflate compression of the container needs, and Expat. Both are available as packages of vcpkg and Conan; their CMake
 * targets and package names differ, which the build hides behind the targets
 * `x3pio_deps::minizip` and `x3pio_deps::expat`.
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake -S . -B build
 * cmake --build build
 * ctest --test-dir build
 * cmake --install build --prefix /path/to/prefix
 * @endcode
 *
 * One test is skipped unless the environment variable `X3PIO_LARGE_TESTS` is
 * set: it writes and reads more than 4 GiB of point data, which needs zip64
 * records, and takes about 30 seconds.
 *
 * With Visual Studio, configure with `-A x64` (or `Win32`, `ARM64`) and name
 * the configuration in the other commands: `--config Release`, and
 * `-C Release` for `ctest`. The tests need libxml2, which checks the files
 * that the writer creates against the schemas of the standard; `-DBUILD_TESTING=OFF`
 * leaves them out.
 *
 * These CMake options control which components are built:
 *
 * <table class="doxtable">
 * <tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Builds</th></tr>
 * <tr><td>X3PIO_BUILD_EXAMPLES</td><td>OFF</td><td>The examples.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>X3PIO_BUILD_EXTERNAL_EXAMPLES</td><td>OFF</td><td>The examples that
 * use external libraries.</td></tr>
 * <tr><td>X3PIO_BUILD_DOCS</td><td>OFF</td><td>This API reference, with
 * Doxygen, as the target `docs`.</td></tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * With Conan, `conan create .` builds and tests the package, and with vcpkg the
 * manifest `vcpkg.json` names the dependencies.
 *
 * @section building_use Using
 * An installed library provides the CMake target `x3pio::x3pio`:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * find_package(x3pio CONFIG REQUIRED)
 * add_executable(main main.c)
 * target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE x3pio::x3pio)
 * @endcode
 *
 * Source files that use the library include `<x3pio/x3pio.h>`, and
 * `<x3pio/file.h>` for the functions that open files. See Examples for typical
 * uses. Without CMake, compile and link with the flags that pkg-config
 * reports:
 * @code{.sh}
 * cc main.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs x3pio) -o main
 * @endcode
 * A static library needs `--static`, which adds minizip-ng and Expat.
 *
 * @section bindings Other Languages
 * The interface is plain C: opaque handles, functions that return a status,
 * structures that are passed by pointer, and no macros that a binding would
 * have to expand. A function that returns a structure by value has a twin that
 * takes a pointer, for a language that cannot take a structure as a return
 * value: x3pio_document_get_axis(), x3pio_document_get_transformation(),
 * x3pio_document_get_dimensions(), x3pio_transformation_init() and
 * x3pio_compression_init(). The program surface.py in `examples/python` writes
 * and reads a file with ctypes.
 *
 * @section layers Three Layers
 * The library offers the points on three levels, from which a program takes the
 * one that suits it:
 * - the values as the file stores them, with the functions x3pio_read_data()
 *   and x3pio_write_data();
 * - the conversion of such values to metres and back, which needs no reader
 *   and no writer, only the document: x3pio_decode_values() and
 *   x3pio_encode_values();
 * - the points in metres, with x3pio_read_points() and x3pio_write_points(),
 *   and the z coordinates of all points in x3pio_read_z() and
 *   x3pio_write_z().
 */

/**
 * @page format x3p Format
 * @details An x3p file is a zip container. The file name extension is `.x3p`.
 * The standard defines these members:
 * - `main.xml` holds the document of the file: the axes, the points and the metadata.
 * - `md5checksum.hex` holds the MD5 checksum of main.xml, the checksums of
 *   the other members follow in main.xml.
 * - `bindata/data.bin` holds the points in the binary format. The text format
 *   puts them into main.xml instead.
 * - `bindata/valid.bin` holds the mask of the points that were measured, which
 *   integer data needs when a point is missing.
 * - The files of vendors, for which the document names an identifier.
 *
 * @section format_revisions Revisions
 * The element `Revision` names the schema of main.xml. A missing or empty
 * element means the first revision, as in the files of early implementations. `ISO5436 - 2000` is the
 * schema of ISO 25178-72:2017, whose calibration record has a required
 * date. `ISO25178-72:2017/DAM1` is the schema of Amendment 1, which
 * requires the data type and the increment of an axis and allows any number of
 * vendor-specific files. A writer writes the revision it is told to and is
 * strict: every file it creates is valid according to the schema of its revision.
 *
 * @section format_axes Axes and Transformation
 * `Record1` holds the feature type: a profile (`PRF`), a surface (`SUR`) or a
 * point cloud (`PCL`), and one axis element for each of the axes CX, CY and CZ. An
 * axis is incremental (`I`) or absolute (`A`). An incremental axis has no
 * stored values: its coordinate is the index of the point times the increment.
 * The values of an absolute axis are stored, as int16, int32, float or double,
 * and a value times the increment of the axis is a length in metres. Offset
 * and rotation do not change the values of the file, they place the points in
 * the coordinate system of the machine:
 * `global = R * (I * stored) + O`. x3pio_read_points() returns the points in
 * the coordinate system of the file, and x3pio_read_points_in() in the
 * global one, with the transformation applied in double precision before the
 * coordinates are stored in the buffers of the program;
 * x3pio_write_points_in() does the reverse. The transformation itself is
 * x3pio_document_transformation().
 *
 * @section format_missing Missing Points
 * A floating-point value that is NaN marks a point that was not measured. An
 * integer has no such value, so the file holds a mask in `valid.bin` with one
 * bit for each point, which is the bit `j % 8` (least significant first) of the
 * byte `j / 8`. A reader gives the mask as a byte for each point, a writer
 * takes it in the same form, see x3pio_validity_pack().
 *
 * @section format_binary Binary Format
 * `data.bin` holds for each point the values of its absolute axes in the order
 * X, Y, Z, as little-endian numbers without padding. The points follow in the
 * order of the standard: u varies fastest, then v, then w, the layer. A matrix
 * is read and written in whole runs along u of `size_x` points, and a list of
 * points in any number of points. The standard contradicts itself on the byte order; the files
 * in use are little-endian, and so are those of the library.
 *
 * @section format_text Text Format
 * The points are `Datum` elements in `DataList` and the values of a point are
 * separated by semicolons; an empty `Datum` is a point that was not measured.
 * ISO 25178-72 recommends the binary format for a dataset of more than 10,000
 * points, because it is more compact and faster to read and write, and
 * explicitly allows it for smaller datasets too. The text format is therefore
 * meant for small datasets, and a reader accepts a larger one without
 * complaint. A text file is bound to main.xml, so a reader parses it as a whole
 * when the file is opened and holds the numbers of all points, about 25 bytes
 * for each point of three axes, and a writer holds them until the file is
 * ended. A reader refuses a main.xml with more than 64 MiB of text in its
 * elements.
 *
 * @section format_vendor Vendor Files
 * A vendor adds files to the container, and registers them by an identifier in
 * main.xml. The two revisions differ in what the identifier stands for:
 * - In Amendment 1 every file has an identifier of its own, a URI with a host
 *   name and a path to a file, and the file lies where the URI says: the labels
 *   of the host name and the segments of the path, with backslashes as
 *   separators, so `http://www.vendor.com/mypath/a.xml` is the member
 *   `www\vendor\com\mypath\a.xml`. The scheme is not part of the name, so
 *   `http://a.b/c` and `https://a.b/c` name the same file, which they share if
 *   its content is the same. A file registered by a new identifier has a host
 *   name without a user name and a port. An identifier without a path names no
 *   file, and a file that no identifier names is not a vendor file.
 * - In ISO 25178-72:2017 a single identifier stands for the extension of the
 *   vendor as a whole, and its files lie in the container under any name except
 *   those of the format. A file with several identifiers is read as Amendment 1.
 *
 * A name is a file or a folder of the container, not both.
 *
 * The functions hide this difference, so that a program serves both revisions
 * with the same calls. A file has an identifier of its own, the vendor, a `/`
 * and the path of the file below it: in Amendment 1 it is the identifier of
 * main.xml, and in 2017 it is made from the one identifier and the name of the
 * file in the container.
 * - x3pio_add_vendor_file() adds a file by its vendor and its path, and
 *   x3pio_add_vendor_file_by_id() by its identifier in full, as a reader
 *   reports it, which is how the files of one file are copied into another.
 *   x3pio_add_vendor_id() registers an identifier without a file.
 * - x3pio_reader_vendor_file_count() and the functions after it list the files
 *   with their identifiers, x3pio_reader_find_vendor_file() finds the next one
 *   below a vendor or a folder of it, and x3pio_read_vendor_file() reads one by
 *   its identifier without reading any other member. x3pio_read_member() reads a
 *   member of the container by its name.
 *
 * ISO 25178-72:2017 cannot take the identifiers of Amendment 1 as they are,
 * since an identifier does not say where the vendor ends and the path begins:
 * a program that converts a file to the first revision names the vendor itself,
 * and adds the files with it.
 *
 * @section format_tolerance Tolerance
 * Real files differ from the standard, and some are damaged. The reader
 * accepts what it can read without doubt, and refuses the rest with an error:
 * - It finds the elements of main.xml by their local name, in any order, with
 *   or without a namespace, and ignores what it does not know. It accepts a
 *   byte order mark.
 * - It takes the defaults of the standard for an optional element that is
 *   missing.
 * - It finds the members in the root of the container or in one folder, as
 *   zipped by hand, and ignores the files that other systems add, such as
 *   `__MACOSX`, `.DS_Store` and `Thumbs.db`, and the entries of folders. It
 *   takes backslashes in the names of members as separators.
 * - It reads the checksum in upper or lower case, with or without a name of
 *   the file after it, and does not check what it cannot read as a checksum,
 *   or a file without a checksum.
 *
 * It refuses these files with X3PIO_ERR_FORMAT: a main.xml that is missing,
 * that is not well-formed or whose root is not an x3p, in several folders or
 * more than one folder deep; a required element that is missing, a data type
 * or an axis type that the standard does not define, a revision that is none
 * of the two, since an unknown revision may store its data differently, a size
 * that is not a whole number, an increment that is zero or infinite; a data file whose length
 * does not fit the dimensions. A checksum that is readable and does not match
 * fails with X3PIO_ERR_CHECKSUM. A program that wants to read such a file
 * regardless turns the check off with x3pio_reader_set_verify().
 */
